cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software in research or write about it, please cite as below."
title: "clearmode"
abstract: >
  CLEAR-100 is a plain-language writing standard and deterministic checker for
  AI-drafted text. It scores any document 0 to 100 against 43 rules on four
  weighted axes: Plain (jargon glossing, reading grade, active voice, short
  words), Human (anti-slop lexicon, punctuation tells, banned openers, sentence
  rhythm), Structured (lists, numbered steps, tables, headings, bold
  discipline), and Dense (the delete test, restatement, throat-clearing
  openers). A single JSON rule pack is the source of truth and compiles to 18
  agent targets, including a Claude Code skill, a Codex AGENTS.md file, a Cursor
  .mdc project rule, a Grok custom instruction, a generic system prompt, and a
  Vale style package, so every surface enforces identical rules without drift.
  The checker is stdlib-only Python with no network calls. It differs from
  anti-slop word lists, which cover vocabulary alone, from ASD-STE100 Simplified
  Technical English, which targets ambiguity for trained technicians and bans
  contractions, and from prose linters such as Vale, which supply an engine but
  no opinion. Four audience profiles set sentence caps and reading-grade targets
  for general, technical, social, and agent readers.
